-- title:
--   Vanishing dichotomy on a fibre of a projective presentation
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field, $X$ a scheme, and $t \colon X \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ a proper morphism. Let $M$ be an object of `X.Modules` which is invertible in the sense of `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ on which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules. Let $N$ be a natural number and let $\mathfrak{P}$ be a `ProjPresentation` of $M$ over $t$ of size $N$, that is: global sections $\sigma_0,\dots,\sigma_N \in \Gamma(M,\top)$, a morphism $\mathfrak{P}.\mathrm{toProj} \colon X \to \operatorname{Proj}$ of the homogeneous coordinate algebra of $k[x_0,\dots,x_N]$ whose composite with the structure projection $\pi$ is $t$, the framing condition that for each $i$ and each open $V$ contained in the preimage of the basic open set $D_+(x_i)$ the map $g \mapsto g \cdot \sigma_i|_V$ is a bijection $\Gamma(X,V) \to \Gamma(M,V)$, and the compatibility asserting that on the preimage of $D_+(x_i)$ the pullback of the ratio $x_j/x_i$ carries $\sigma_i$ to $\sigma_j$. Let $Z \subseteq X$ be closed and irreducible, let $y$ be a point of that $\operatorname{Proj}$ with $\{y\}$ closed, and suppose $Z$ is contained in the preimage of $\{y\}$ under the underlying map of $\mathfrak{P}.\mathrm{toProj}$. Then for every morphism $s \colon \mathbf{1}_{X.\mathrm{Modules}} \to M$, either $Z$ is contained in the support of `Scheme.Modules.zeroSchemeIdeal s` or $Z$ is disjoint from it, where `zeroSchemeIdeal s` is the infimum of those ideal sheaf data $J$ on $X$ whose ideal on each affine open $U$ contains the ideal generated by the coefficients of $s$ on $U$.
--
--   This is the dichotomy, in the style of Mumford's rigidity arguments, that a global section of an invertible module either vanishes identically along an irreducible closed subset contracted by a projective presentation or vanishes nowhere on it. It is used in the proof that such a presentation has finite fibres once the locus of sections vanishing after pullback is finite.
